    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d360">

                <pb facs="0558" n="272" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg272"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.622a/150</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-06-09">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 9, 1916, noon</hi>
                        </date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 10, 4.50 p.
                        m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3978. Von Jagow again asked me why notes
                    sent to Great Britain about the <hi rendition="#italic">China</hi> and post
                    [mail] were not published, as it would make good impression here. He said that
                    he hoped President and you would not be affected by what was said in the
                    Reichstag against the peace mediation of the President.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d360-f1"/> He said that I knew the position of Government here.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d360-f1" n="1" xml:id="d360-f1-content">
                <p>See the Ambassador’s telegram No. 3941, May 31, 1916, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg33">p. 33</ref>.</p>
            </note>
